{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>You shall not oppress, etc.</b> The intention is that since HaShem commanded concerning one who takes another's money without his knowledge, a person should not say that He was particular only about the manner of theft — but robbery and oppression where the person knows about them have no warning. Therefore He returned to command also about one who takes his fellow's money with his knowledge, by coercion; and not appropriation of money by outright force alone, but even withholding a laborer's wage is forbidden." "And the reason for His saying <b>your fellow</b> is to remove the claim of his being a friend — and through this he would lay hand on his money and say: since he is a friend, like a brother to him, between himself and his own heart he will forgive him and will not wish to punish him. And since this warning concerns only matters between a person and his fellow — when he forgives, there is no offense — therefore He said <b>you shall not oppress your fellow.</b>" "<b>And by way of</b> allusion, it alludes to the way the Sages interpreted (Berakhot 35b) the verse ״גּוֹזֵל אָבִיו וְאִמּוֹ״ etc. — this refers to one who benefits from this world without a blessing, end quote. And this is the statement of HaShem here: <b>you shall not oppress your fellow</b> — this is the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is written (Proverbs 27:10): ״רֵיעֲךָ וְרֵיעַ אָבִיךָ״ — that he should not rob the blessings he is obligated to recite over what he benefits from in this world. And His saying <b>and you shall not rob</b> is an allusion that even in this oppression there is robbery against others, which is what the verse ״גּוֹזֵל אָבִיו וְאִמּוֹ״ alluded to, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted there: אָבִיו — this is the Holy One, blessed be He; אִמּוֹ — this is Knesset Israel; for when a person diminishes a mitzvah that requires performance, it causes harm also to the collective of Israel. And He said <b>it shall not remain overnight</b> — that one must give a daily portion, each day, along the lines of what they said of blessed memory (Vayikra Rabbah 26:4): they would borrow by day and repay by night. And this is what is alluded to here: ״לֹא תָלִין פְּעֻלַּת שָׂכִיר <b>אִתְּךָ</b>״ — the meaning of the word אִתְּךָ refers back to the laborer, which is the spirit of HaShem that He placed in man as a hired worker, as a sojourner, and his wage is the performance of HaShem's mitzvot." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes along the lines of what they said of blessed memory, that Torah scholars have no livelihood in this world — as they said (Yalkut Shimoni on Proverbs, remez 974): the Torah said, for what reason are My children poor? And the reason — I heard a correct reason from the Arizal, of blessed memory: the world cannot bear the abundance of goodness that would be given on behalf of the righteous; therefore He channels only through the intermediate ones, and by that path Torah scholars are sustained. From this you learn that the rich are nothing but a conduit prepared for the supply of the needs of the righteous. And HaShem commanded that a person should not oppress his fellow — who is the righteous one, as it is written (Psalms 122:8): ״לְמַעַן אַחַי וְרֵעָי״ — and he should not give him his allotted portion, and this oppression is well-known." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes that one should not oppress the word et that appears by ״רֵעֲךָ״ — which is the Holy One, blessed be He, as Rabbi Akiva expounded (Pesachim 22b): ״אֶת יְהֹוָה אֱלֹהֶיךָ תִּירָא״ — to include Torah scholars. And the intention in this is to fear the sages who judge on earth, and since this is so, behold there are judges — אֱלֹהִים — judging on earth whom one should fear, to fulfill the matter of justice; and through this he will not rob, for the judge will remove the stolen property from him."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:59:14.896947074Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}